# GitHub CODEOWNERS matches by file path only, last matching rule wins -- ordered here from
# broadest fallback to most specific override. See org-model/main.tf for how the directories
# below get composed, and the comment there for onboarding a new team.
#
# Replace the example @org/... handles below with your real GitHub team slugs.

# Fallback: platform (the schema/mechanism owner) owns anything under org-model/ not
# explicitly assigned below.
/org-model/                          @org/platform-team

# Team-owned data: each team's people/roles/authority-lines/components, at every level of the
# hierarchy that has its own head (departments included, not just leaf squads). teams/<team>/
# directories nest to mirror the org chart (a team's directory sits inside its parent_team's),
# but CODEOWNERS matches by path prefix with last-match-wins, so nesting does NOT imply a parent
# team's line covers its descendants -- add one explicit line per team, at its full nested path,
# as new teams/.../<team>/ directories are created. None exist yet in this template, see
# /examples/ for worked patterns.

# common/ is organizational *content* (the root team record, the type vocabulary), not
# engineering -- owned by the business side, not platform. validated/ (schema/validation) and
# main.tf (module composition) stay platform-owned since those are mechanism, not content.
/org-model/common/                   @org/people-ops
/org-model/validated/                @org/platform-team
/org-model/main.tf                   @org/platform-team

# Illustrative only -- not real org data, owned by whoever maintains the framework itself.
/examples/                           @org/platform-team

# Everything outside org-model/ (ADRs, docs, the public-facing README) stays with platform
# for now.
/docs/                               @org/platform-team
/CONTEXT.md                          @org/platform-team
/README.md                           @org/platform-team
